  "hash": "e2d610928e0fb19dd5b48d952767bfef",
  "result": {
    "engine": "knitr",
    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Maps and Choropleths\"\nformat:\n  html:\n    code-copy: true\n    theme: cosmo\n    toc: true\n    lightbox: auto\nexecute:\n  warning: false\n  message: false\n  fig-width: 7\n  fig-height: 5\n---\n\n\n\nA **choropleth** colours each area by a value. It is the most common map in public health and also the easiest to get wrong. This page covers **rates versus counts**, **classification**, colour choice and an interactive version.\n\n## Counts mislead, rates do not\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\np1 <- ggplot(blocks) + geom_sf(aes(fill = dengue_cases)) + scale_fill_viridis_c(name = \"Cases\") + labs(title = \"Dengue cases (counts)\")\np2 <- ggplot(blocks) + geom_sf(aes(fill = dengue_per_100k)) + scale_fill_viridis_c(name = \"per 100,000\", option = \"magma\", direction = -1) + labs(title = \"Dengue per 100,000 (rate)\")\npatchwork::wrap_plots(p1, p2)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n![](maps_choropleth_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-2-1.png){width=960}\n:::\n:::\n\n\nCounts follow **population size**; a populous block looks bad even when its risk is average. Map **rates** (or standardised ratios) for risk, counts for workload.\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nblocks |> st_drop_geometry() |>\n  summarise(r_cases_pop = cor(dengue_cases, population), r_rate_pop = cor(dengue_per_100k, population)) |> round(2)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n\n```\n  r_cases_pop r_rate_pop\n1        0.67       0.16\n```\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n## Classification changes the story\n\nContinuous colour hides nothing but is hard to read; **classes** are easier but are a choice. Three common schemes (package `classInt`):\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nlibrary(classInt)\ncls <- function(x, style) cut(x, classIntervals(x, 5, style = style)$brks, include.lowest = TRUE)\nlong <- bind_rows(\n  mutate(blocks, scheme = \"Equal interval\", cls = cls(dengue_per_100k, \"equal\")),\n  mutate(blocks, scheme = \"Quantile (equal counts)\", cls = cls(dengue_per_100k, \"quantile\")),\n  mutate(blocks, scheme = \"Natural breaks (Jenks)\", cls = cls(dengue_per_100k, \"jenks\")))\nggplot(long) + geom_sf(aes(fill = cls), colour = \"white\", linewidth = 0.1) + facet_wrap(~ scheme) +\n  scale_fill_brewer(palette = \"YlOrRd\", name = \"per 100,000\") + theme(legend.position = \"bottom\", axis.text = element_blank())\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n![](maps_choropleth_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-4-1.png){width=960}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n-   **Equal interval:** honest about the scale, but a skewed variable puts most areas in one class.\n-   **Quantile:** every class has the same number of areas, so there is always a \"worst 20%\" even if all are similar.\n-   **Natural breaks:** groups similar values; breaks depend on the data, so maps are hard to compare.\n\nState the scheme and the breaks in the caption. For comparing maps, use **the same fixed breaks**.\n\n## Colour\n\n-   Use a **sequential** palette (light to dark) for rates; a **diverging** palette only when there is a meaningful midpoint (e.g. ratio to the district average = 1).\n-   Avoid rainbow palettes; check that the map reads in grey-scale and for colour-blind viewers (`viridis` is safe).\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nblocks$rr <- blocks$dengue_per_100k / (sum(blocks$dengue_cases) / sum(blocks$population) * 1e5)\nggplot(blocks) + geom_sf(aes(fill = rr)) +\n  scale_fill_gradient2(low = \"#2166ac\", mid = \"white\", high = \"#b2182b\", midpoint = 1, name = \"Rate ratio\\nvs district\") +\n  labs(title = \"Diverging scale centred on the district rate\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n![](maps_choropleth_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-5-1.png){width=672}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n## Layers, labels and the finishing touches\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nfac <- read_csv(\"../data/gis/facilities.csv\", show_col_types = FALSE) |> st_as_sf(coords = c(\"easting_m\", \"northing_m\"), crs = 32644) |> st_transform(4326)\nggplot(blocks) + geom_sf(aes(fill = dengue_per_100k), colour = \"white\", linewidth = 0.15) +\n  geom_sf(data = fac, aes(shape = type), size = 2) +\n  scale_fill_distiller(palette = \"Oranges\", direction = 1, name = \"Dengue per\\n100,000\") +\n  labs(title = \"Dengue incidence and health facilities\", subtitle = \"Synthetic district, 79 blocks\", shape = \"Facility\") +\n  theme(axis.text = element_blank(), panel.grid = element_blank())\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n![](maps_choropleth_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-6-1.png){width=672}\n:::\n:::\n\n\nGood maps carry a **title, a legend with units, the data source, and a north arrow / scale bar when the area matters** (`ggspatial::annotation_scale()` and `annotation_north_arrow()`).\n\n## Interactive map\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nlibrary(leaflet)\npal <- colorNumeric(\"YlOrRd\", blocks$dengue_per_100k)\nleaflet(blocks) |> addProviderTiles(\"CartoDB.Positron\") |>\n  addPolygons(fillColor = ~pal(dengue_per_100k), fillOpacity = 0.8, weight = 1, color = \"white\",\n              label = ~paste0(block_name, \": \", dengue_per_100k, \" per 100,000\"),\n              highlightOptions = highlightOptions(weight = 3, color = \"black\", bringToFront = TRUE)) |>\n  addLegend(pal = pal, values = ~dengue_per_100k, title = \"Dengue per 100,000\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"leaflet html-widget html-fill-item\" id=\"htmlwidget-2a5100cb6463399fb499\" style=\"width:100%;height:464px;\"></div>\n<script type=\"application/json\" data-for=\"htmlwidget-2a5100cb6463399fb499\">{\"x\":{\"options\":{\"crs\":{\"crsClass\":\"L.CRS.EPSG3857\",\"code\":null,\"proj4def\":null,\"projectedBounds\":null,\"options\":{}}},\"calls\":[{\"method\":\"addProviderTiles\",\"args\":[\"CartoDB.Positron\",null,null,{\"errorTileUrl\":\"\",\"noWrap\":false,\"detectRetina\":false}]},{\"method\":\"addPolygons\",\"args\":[[[[{\"lng\":[80.8480328814113,80.76897733094752,80.71710266417162,80.71289109840583,80.85228831939297,80.84803